d against the silent floor.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback format="html"><text /></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0.0" format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;Please do not read that out loud,&amp;quot; I whispered.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback format="html"><text /></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0.0" format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;Around me, every chair scraped back at once.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback format="html"><text /></feedback></answer></question><question type="multichoice"><name><text>CW_U02_L01_R_Q24</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;In a draft, a character is searching a room for a missing letter. The next sentence says, &amp;quot;She left.&amp;quot; Readers cannot tell why she stopped searching. Which revision advice best addresses that gap?&lt;/p&gt;</text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text /></generalfeedback><defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade><penalty>0.3333333</penalty><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answernumbering>none</answernumbering><showstandardinstruction>1</showstandardinstruction><correctfeedback format="html"><text /></correctfeedback><partiallycorrectfeedback format="html"><text /></partiallycorrectfeedback><incorrectfeedback format="html"><text /></incorrectfeedback><answer fraction="0.0" format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;Replace left with departed; the reason will then be clear.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback format="html"><text /></feedback></answer><answer fraction="100.0" format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;Show the discovery that causes the departure and connect it to the action.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback format="html"><text /></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0.0" format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;Describe the room&amp;#39;s furniture in more detail before the departure.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback format="html"><text /></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0.0" format="html"><text>&lt;p&gt;Add three time markers but leave the reason for leaving unstated.&lt;/p&gt;</text><feedback fo